## 4. History - so a later session does not re-litigate this

Append-only. Every state change, with the date and the reason, including the
reversals.

| Date | Lane | Change | Why |
|---|---|---|---|
| <YYYY_MM_DD> | <LANE_NAME> | <ADDED_OR_CANCELLED_OR_REVERSED> | <REASON> |

Two of these rows in opposite directions within a week is not embarrassing; it
is the record that stops a third round. **A cancelled lane's record stays so it
is not re-adopted by amnesia** - without the history, the next evaluation reads
the marketing page, adopts it, and rediscovers the same disqualifier at the
same cost.

---

## 5. Salvage - a dead lane's findings outlive it

**A cancelled scanner's real findings are worked, not discarded with it.** The
lane's verdict was about the lane: its metering, its scoping, its output
format. It was never a verdict on the defects it found.

| Finding class the dead lane covered | What covers it now |
|---|---|
| <FINDING_CLASS> | <REPLACEMENT_MECHANISM_OR_NOTHING_YET> |

A row whose right-hand side is "nothing yet" is honest and is a gap on the
backlog. A row quietly deleted along with the lane is how a whole class of
defect stops being looked for and nobody can name the day it happened.

---

## 6. Maintenance

Written by `/reviewer-integration`: `add <lane>` creates or updates the lane's
block, `remove <lane>` records the cancellation with its disqualifiers and the
owner actions owed, `baseline <lane>` reconciles the block against the live
config and reports drift. Hand edits are fine; the next `baseline` will
reconcile them.
